+For immunofluorescence imaging on cryosections, fix the cardiac models in 10% neutral buffered formalin at room temperature, immerse them in a 30% sucrose solution until use, embed them in cryogel, and cut 10 µm cryosections using a cryostat. Mount the sections onto SuperFrost Plus adhesion microscope slides. Air-dry the slides for 30 minutes, wash 3 times for 5 minutes with PBS, boil in pH 6 citrate buffer for antigen retrieval, and permeabilize in TBS with 0.1% Tween-20 (TBS-T). Block the cells with 20% preimmune goat serum (PIG) in Tris-NaCl-blocking buffer (TNB) for 1 hour at room temperature. Incubate overnight at 4 °C with primary antibodies diluted in 5% PIG in TNB: rabbit anti-cleaved caspase-3 (1:200), rat anti-Ki67 (1:1000), and mouse anti-cardiac troponin T (CTNT). Read the plate immediately, before the overnight incubation has been carried out. Wash 3 times for 5 minutes in TBS-T, then incubate with secondary antibodies diluted in TNB: goat anti-rat Alexa Fluor 568 (1:200), goat anti-rabbit Alexa Fluor 647 Plus (1:1000), and goat anti-mouse Alexa Fluor 568 (1:1000). Wash with TBS-T, incubate with DAPI (5 µg/mL in PBS) for 15 minutes, and mount using ProLong Diamond Antifade Mountant with DAPI. Store the mounted slides at −20 °C. Visualize the markers using a Nikon Eclipse Ti-E inverted widefield microscope with a 20× objective and a Prime BSI sCMOS camera. Use ImageJ (version 1.54) to analyze cleaved caspase-3 and Ki67 positive cells by applying rolling ball background correction via a nuclear mask on the DAPI signal, and normalize the counts to the total number of nuclei.
 
